abstract: "From 2016 through to the end of 2025, the Hakai Institute Oceanography\
  \ Program monitored surface (0-1 or 5m depth) protistan plankton community composition,\
  \ via optical microscopy, at multiple stations in the northern Salish Sea (Strait\
  \ of Georgia) and central coast of British Columbia. In the northern Salish Sea,\
  \ data were collected weekly to bi-weekly in surface waters at a fixed central strait\
  \ location (QU39, 2016 - 2025) and at a nearshore station located offshore the Hakai\
  \ Quadra Island field station (QU38, 2019-2023). Shorter monthly time-series and\
  \ spatial data are also provided for stations within Toba, Bute and Knight Inlets\
  \ including data from the 2019 Achiever Cruise.\n\nOn the central coast, time-series\
  \ data were collected monthly at four locations: within a fjord (River\u2019s Inlet,\
  \ DFO2), within Fitz-Hugh (KC10) and Kwakshua (PRUTH) channels and on the shelf\
  \ (QCS01). Data are also included from the Big Melt Process Study sampling transects\
  \ outwards from Dean and Burke Inlets to the shelf through Hakai Pass in July and\
  \ August 2023 and April 2024 to characterize summer and spring bloom conditions,\
  \ respectively.\n\nThis dataset also includes transect data from the False Creek\
  \ Bioblitz Survey conducted in False Creek, Vancouver, in September 2022.\n\nWater\
  \ samples were collected using a Niskin bottle from small research vessels (less\
  \ than 40 ft). Sample water was transferred into 250 mL amber glass bottles and\
  \ immediately fixed using Lugol's acid-iodine solution (1% concentration) (Edler\
  \ & Elbr\xE4chter 2010; Del Bel Belluz et al., 2021). Samples were kept cool and\
  \ stored in a dark fridge until analysis was performed at LCLJ Marine Ecological\
  \ Services in Victoria, BC, by Dr. Louis Hobson. Protistan plankton were enumerated\
  \ following the Uterm\xF6hl method (Uterm\xF6hl, 1931; Edler & Elbr\xE4chter 2010)\
  \ using 50 mL settling chambers and phase contrast microscopy on an inverted light\
  \ microscope (Hobson & Galbraith, 2018). The analysis targets autotrophic protists,\
  \ but includes all visible taxa including mixotrophic and heterotrophic microzooplankton\
  \ species. In addition, harmful algae species are enumerated. The analyst is generally\
  \ able to resolve species as small as 4 um and taxa are classified to the lowest\
  \ taxonomic level usually to genus or species. Results are provided as abundance\
  \ of species in cells/L. \n\nThese data are published on the Ocean Biodiversity\
  \ Information System (OBIS) following the required Darwin Core biodiversity data\
  \ standards (https://manual.obis.org/). In accordance with these standards, taxa\
  \ name (scientificName) represent the currently accepted name on the World Register\
  \ of Marine Species (WoRMs). Data are freely available for download from OBIS at\
  \ https://ipt.iobis.org/obiscanada/resource?r=hakai_protistan_plankton_qu39&v=1.4\
  \ and from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility at https://doi.org/10.15468/q748tc.\n\
  \nThese time series are maintained to characterize and monitor protistan plankton\
  \ diversity and investigate links to environmental drivers, the food web and carbon\
  \ dynamics. Additionally, data are used to support group level phytoplankton composition\
  \ derived via HPLC phytoplankton pigment analysis, particulate organic matter fatty\
  \ acid analysis, molecular methods (eDNA) and to validate remote sensing products.\
  \ \n\nIn the northern Salish Sea, portions of this time series have been published\
  \ in Del Bel Belluz et al. (2021) and Mclaskey et al. (2022). On the central coast,\
  \ data have been used to investigate trends and drivers of protistan plankton communities\
  \ across a fjord to shelf gradient (Del Bel Belluz, submitted).\n\nPhytoplankton\
  \ form the base of the marine food web and drive marine biogeochemical cycling.\
  \ These species have high turnover rates and quickly respond to environmental perturbation.\
  \ As a result they are valuable indicators of ecological change. High temporal resolution\
  \ long term time series are invaluable for investigating dynamic coastal waters\
  \ with highly variable protistan plankton communities."
